    excerpt: `The most powerful piece of technology in my town is a Google Sheet with forty-seven rows, each one representing a family that has something to give and something they need. I built it during the winter of junior year, after the pharmacy in town closed and Mrs. Hendricks told my mother she couldn't get her insulin because the nearest Walgreens was forty minutes away and her car wouldn't start in the cold. My mother drove her. The next week, Mrs. Hendricks dropped off a dozen eggs from her chickens. No one arranged this. It was just how things worked.

I wondered if it could work at scale. The Google Sheet was simple: column A, what you can offer (rides, childcare, firewood, meals, equipment). Column B, what you need. Column C, contact info. I printed flyers and left them at the post office, the church, and the gas station. Within a month, forty-seven families had signed up. Mr. Olsen, who owns a backhoe, traded driveway plowing for piano lessons from Mrs. Chen. The Garcias, who raise goats, traded milk for school supply pickups from anyone driving to the Walmart in the next county.

The spreadsheet has facilitated over 200 exchanges in nine months. No money changes hands. There is no central authority deciding what anything is worth. The system works because each participant decides the value of what they give and receive, and the community self-corrects when someone takes more than they offer. This is, I've since learned, called a gift economy. I didn't know the term when I built it. I just knew that Mrs. Hendricks needed insulin and my mother had a car.`,
