    excerpt: `I started with a cardboard box in the girls' bathroom and a Sharpie sign that said "Take What You Need," and within six months I was testifying before the school board about menstrual equity. The box was not an activism project. It was a response to the fact that Maya Torres missed a day of school every month because she couldn't afford pads, and nobody was talking about it because periods are the kind of problem that exists in silence.

I stocked the box with products I bought with my babysitting money: $23 per month, roughly forty pads and twenty tampons. Within two weeks, the box was empty. I restocked it. Empty again. I started tracking: usage rates, product preferences, the days of the month when demand spiked. The data showed that approximately thirty students were relying on a cardboard box in a bathroom for a basic health need.

I brought the data to the principal. She was sympathetic but said the budget didn't allow for it. I asked how much the school spent on toilet paper annually. She said $4,200. I said that menstrual products for the same population would cost approximately $1,800 per year, less than half the toilet paper budget. She paused. "I've never thought about it that way," she said. I said, "That's because no one was supposed to." The school board approved the funding three months later, and twelve bathrooms in our district now stock free menstrual products. The cardboard box is retired. The Sharpie sign is in my desk drawer.`,
